This landing page has 'Poor' CLS in field data:

```html
<style>
  @font-face {
    font-family: 'Brand';
    src: url('/fonts/brand.woff2') format('woff2');
  }
  body { font-family: 'Brand', sans-serif; }
</style>

<div id="promo-banner"></div>
<img src="/hero.jpg" alt="Hero">
<p>Some intro copy that renders immediately on load.</p>

<script>
  fetch('/api/promo').then(res => res.json()).then(data => {
    document.querySelector('#promo-banner').innerHTML = `<div class="banner">${data.message}</div>`;
  });
</script>
```

Identify at least three distinct issues contributing to layout shift, and explain how to fix each.

This is the above-the-fold hero section of a React marketing page. Field LCP is consistently 'Poor' (>4s), even though the hero image itself is small and well-optimized (80KB):

```jsx
function HeroSection({ heroImageUrl }) {
  return (
    <section className="hero">
      <img src={heroImageUrl} loading="lazy" alt="Product hero" />
      <h1>Welcome to Acme</h1>
    </section>
  );
}

function App() {
  const [heroImageUrl, setHeroImageUrl] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/hero-config')
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(data => setHeroImageUrl(data.imageUrl));
  }, []);

  if (!heroImageUrl) return <div className="hero-placeholder" />;

  return <HeroSection heroImageUrl={heroImageUrl} />;
}
```

Diagnose all root causes of the LCP regression and rewrite the code to fix them.

questions.correctAnswer:
Root cause 1 — `loading="lazy"` on the LCP candidate itself: this tells the browser to defer fetching until the image is near the viewport, which is exactly backwards for the single most important above-the-fold image; it should never be lazy-loaded.

Root cause 2 — client-side discovery waterfall: the image URL isn't known until JS loads → hydrates → `useEffect` fires → `fetch('/api/hero-config')` resolves → state updates → `<img>` finally renders → the browser can start fetching the image. This chains multiple sequential round trips before the LCP resource request is even issued, and because the URL never appears in the initial server-rendered HTML, the browser's preload scanner can't discover it early either.

Root cause 3 — no priority hint: even once rendered, there's no `fetchpriority="high"` to tell the browser this is a critical resource.

Fix — resolve the hero config server-side (SSR) so the URL is present in the initial HTML response and can be preloaded, drop `loading="lazy"`, and add `fetchpriority="high"`:

```jsx
// Server-rendered: heroImageUrl resolved before the HTML is sent, passed as a prop — no client fetch/waterfall.
function HeroSection({ heroImageUrl }) {
  return (
    <section className="hero">
      <img src={heroImageUrl} fetchpriority="high" alt="Product hero" />
      <h1>Welcome to Acme</h1>
    </section>
  );
}

function App({ heroImageUrl }) {
  return <HeroSection heroImageUrl={heroImageUrl} />;
}

// In the document <head>, rendered server-side, for earliest possible discovery:
// <link rel="preload" as="image" href={heroImageUrl} fetchpriority="high" />
```

questions.question:
`products` contains 5,000 items. Typing in the filter input feels laggy — the DevTools Performance panel shows long tasks over 200ms on every keystroke, and field INP is 'Poor' (>500ms):

```jsx
function ProductList({ products }) {
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');
  const [filteredProducts, setFilteredProducts] = useState(products);

  const handleInputChange = (e) => {
    const value = e.target.value;
    setFilter(value);

    logAnalyticsEvent('filter_changed', { value });
    const filtered = products
      .filter(p => p.name.toLowerCase().includes(value.toLowerCase()))
      .map(p => ({ ...p, score: computeRelevanceScore(p, value) }))
      .sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score);

    setFilteredProducts(filtered);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input value={filter} onChange={handleInputChange} />
      <ProductGrid products={filteredProducts} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

Diagnose why INP is poor here and rewrite the code to fix it.

questions.correctAnswer:
Diagnosis: every single keystroke synchronously runs an analytics call plus an O(n log n) filter + map + sort over 5,000 items inside the input's event handler, all before React can commit and paint the updated input character. This inflates the 'processing time' phase of the interaction well past the frame budget, so both the visible input update and the overall interaction blow past the 200ms/500ms INP thresholds — the typed character itself feels janky because its paint is blocked behind the expensive, unrelated recompute.

Fix — separate the urgent update (what the user sees typed) from the expensive, deferrable update (the filtered/sorted list) using React 18's `useTransition`, so React can paint the new input value immediately while the heavy recompute runs as an interruptible, lower-priority transition; also move analytics off the critical path and (ideally) debounce or index the search rather than re-scanning the full array on every keystroke:

```jsx
function ProductList({ products }) {
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');
  const [filteredProducts, setFilteredProducts] = useState(products);
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

  const handleInputChange = (e) => {
    const value = e.target.value;
    setFilter(value); // urgent: keeps the input itself responsive

    startTransition(() => {
      const filtered = products
        .filter(p => p.name.toLowerCase().includes(value.toLowerCase()))
        .map(p => ({ ...p, score: computeRelevanceScore(p, value) }))
        .sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score);
      setFilteredProducts(filtered);
    });

    queueMicrotask(() => logAnalyticsEvent('filter_changed', { value }));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input value={filter} onChange={handleInputChange} />
      <ProductGrid products={filteredProducts} loading={isPending} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

For very large or frequently re-filtered datasets, also consider debouncing the expensive recompute and/or moving it to a Web Worker or a pre-built search index so it never occupies the main thread during the interaction at all.
